    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, the `disable` command is used to disable an interface (equivalent to Cisco's `shutdown`). To enable an interface (equivalent to Cisco's `no shutdown`), you would typically omit the `disable` statement or use `delete interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable`. However, since you asked for the translation of `no shutdown` (which enables the interface), the Juniper equivalent would be to ensure the interface isn't disabled, hence the `delete` command would be more accurate. \\n\\nFor a more precise translation of `no shutdown`:\\n##\\ndelete interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nBut since the original request was for a direct command, the first answer shows the inverse (disable). The second block shows the true equivalent of `no shutdown`.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, the `disable` command is used to disable an interface (equivalent to Cisco's `shutdown`). To enable an interface (equivalent to Cisco's `no shutdown`), you would typically omit the `disable` statement or use `delete interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable`. However, since you asked for the translation of `no shutdown` (which enables the interface), the Juniper equivalent would be to ensure the interface isn't disabled, hence the `delete` command would be more accurate. \\n\\nFor a more precise translation of `no shutdown`:\\n##\\ndelete interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nBut since the original request was for a direct command, the first answer shows the inverse (d