    "['username [parameter1] privilege [parameter2] secret [parameter3] [parameter4]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set system login user client002 class super-user authentication plain-text-password', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set system login user client002 class super-user authentication plain-text-password', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco username configuration command:\\n\\n##\\nset system login user client002 class super-user authentication plain-text-password\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. \"privilege 3\" in Cisco translates to \"class super-user\" in Juniper (super-user being the highest privilege level)\\n2. Juniper will prompt you to enter the password interactively after executing this command, similar to Cisco\\'s \"secret 0\" followed by the plaintext password\\n3. For actual deployment, you would typically use encrypted passwords rather than plain-text in Juniper (via \"authentication encrypted-password\" with a hash)\\n\\nAlternative if you need to provide the password directly in the configuration:\\n##\\nset system login user client002 class super-user authentication plain-text-password \"<password_for_client002>\"\\n##\\n\\n(Though storing plaintext passwords in configuration is not recommended for security reasons)', 'trans_template': 'set system login user client002 class super-user authentication plain-text-password'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set system login user client002 class super-user authentication plain-text-password \"<password_for_client002>\"', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set system login user client002 class super-user authentication plain-text-password \"<password_for_client002>\"', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco username configuration command:\\n\\n##\\nset system login user client002 class super-user authentication plain-text-password\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. \"privilege 3\" in Cisco translates to \"class super-user\" in Juniper (super-user being the highest privilege level)\\n2. Juniper will prompt you to enter the password interactively after executing this command, similar to Cisco\\'s \"secret 0\" followed by the plaintext password\\n3. For actual deployment, you would typically use encrypted passwords rather than plain-text in Juniper (via \"authentication encrypted-password\" with a hash)\\n\\nAlternative if you need to provide the password directly in the configuration:\\n##\\nset system login user client002 class super-user authentication plain-text-password \"<password_for_client002>\"\\n##\\n\\n(Though storing plaintext passwords in configuration is not recommended for security reasons)', 'trans_template': 'set system login user client002 class super-user authentication plain-text-password \"<password_for_client002>\"'}]]": 1,
